 # Registry Design
 
-## Context
+## Context and Design Philosophy
 
-Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest.
+Technicians enter an equipment serial number and need its calibration date, due date, and equipment description. A signed snapshot contains newline-delimited JSON records, a schema version, a snapshot date, and a signature manifest. The kiosk has no network route during normal operation, so every lookup must resolve from files on the kiosk alone.
 
 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface and never reads registry storage directly.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+The lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index that lives beside the active snapshot inside this node. Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node; no other component touches the index file. When a snapshot arrives, this node validates it, builds the index from the validated records, and swaps it in atomically with the snapshot. Lookups run as ordinary SQL queries against the embedded database — no service process, no network, no timeout handling beyond local file access.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Build the SQLite index from the validated records; a parse or validation failure never reaches the index. Activate the new snapshot and its index together only after all validation and index construction succeed — a lookup never observes a half-built index or a snapshot whose index is missing. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot and its index active and reports a useful error.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant — the stored serial is indexed as-is and the normalized form is used for matching. Return the snapshot date, taken from the active snapshot's manifest, in both `Found` and `NotFound` results. A lookup against a missing or unreadable index reports an error rather than returning `NotFound`, so a broken import cannot masquerade as "no such serial".
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index built from each validated snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Lookups stay inside the tenet that all lookup completes from kiosk files with no runtime network access. Each kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on snapshot arrival, which keeps that cost inside this node and keeps the certificates interface unchanged. The HTTPS directory was rejected because registry lookups would depend on the lab network and need service timeout handling; the managed search service was rejected because recurring cost, credentials, and external connectivity would become registry concerns and conflict with the isolated-network tenets. |
+| Index activation | Swap snapshot and index together after full validation | In-place incremental index updates | Keeps the atomic-activation guarantee simple: either the previous pair or the new pair is visible, never a mix. A 90,000-record rebuild is fast enough at snapshot frequency (USB-delivered, not live sync). |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Lookup backend is a local embedded SQLite index; selection criteria and rejected alternatives recorded above.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Index file location and naming on the kiosk filesystem — decide during implementation.
+2. Whether the SQLite schema version tracks the snapshot schema version or evolves independently — decide when the first schema change actually occurs.
